arginal tax rates of like 90 percent  but he had them at a time where the  were a ton of mechanisms to avoid  actually paying a high effective tax  rate if you were very wealthy so when  you look back at the what matters is the  effective tax rate if you look back at  the Eisenhower era even though you had  those high marginal tax rates the top 1%  of taxpayers in the 50s we're only  paying about 42% total of their income  in taxes so as a result the tax burden  on high-income households today is only  slightly lower than what those same top  1% paid in the 1950s and what Joe Biden  is proposing to do could raise taxes on  the rich well beyond what they were  under the Eisenhower 91% top marginal  tax rate days because as you can see  since the early 90s the effective tax  rate on the top 1% has bounced between  35 and 40 percent okay so what will Joe  Biden's tax plan do number one yes it  will undo those Trump brackets that are  similar to the george w bush brackets  the top tax rate will go from 37 percent  to 39.6 percent it's the right move it's  not that big of a thing for a large  swath of Americans the very important  change that Biden would make is that  earnings above 400,000 would be subject  to the Social Security tax of 12.1  percent  this is monstrous in terms of the total  amount of money right now you stopped  paying Social Security tax at about a  hundred and thirty seven thousand  dollars in income you pay it on your  first hundred and thirty seven K then it  goes to zero what Biden proposes is  there's a doughnut hole there between a  hundred and thirty seven and four  hundred thousand where you don't pay  that twelve percent tax but then when  you get to four hundred thousand dollars  in income and going all the way up you  pay twelve point one percent that is  absolutely massive in terms of the  amount of money that would collect and  that money would be used to increase the  social security benefits for the poor so  that would actually remove hundreds of  thousands of seniors from poverty it  costs the middle class  zero and it comes from people earning  over 400,000 now you might correctly say  but but David hold on a second sir  as Donald Trump says David a lot of the  rich are the non-working rich they're  not earning wages so they wouldn't be  affected by that and you're completely  correct the other thing that Joe Biden's  tax plan would do is remove the lower  capital gains rate on dividends and the  long-term capital gains rate if you make  over 1 million dollars per year so the  non-working millionaires would be paying  in dramatically more money because they  would have to pay their income tax rate  rather than the capital gains rate on a  whole bunch of dividends and capital  gains this could double the tax rate for  non-working millionaires who get most of  their income from investments also very  importantly Biden is calling for a full  repeal of stepped-up basis now what did  this requires a little bit of  explanation but what this means is if  someone donates imagine that someone  buys a thousand dollars worth of stock  in the 1970s they die and they give you  a million dollars worth of stock that's  a huge gain right it went from a  thousand to a million dollars there's a  cartoonish example what the stepped-up  basis allows is that if you is the heir  with that stock decide to sell it at a  time when it's worth 1 point 1 million  dollars stepped-up basis allows you to  only pay gains on the gain from the time  you inherited the stock to the time that  you sell it so even though the person  who gave you the stock the stock went  from a thousand dollars to a million  dollars in value when you got it it was  worth a million and it went up to 1.1  you only pay capital gains on that  hundred thousand dollars from a million  to one point one that is a massive  massive handout on which taxes could be  levied and it is not and it is estimated  to be if a Joe Biden were to eliminate  the the stepped-up basis  provision that would be more than four  trillion dollars of a tax increase on  the rich so joe biden's tax plan  actually does a lot of good now let's be  thorough it doesn't do a lot of things  that we should be looking at that other  candidates have proposed joe biden has  not proposed the financial transaction  tax a little slice which could be used  to fund all sorts of important programs  joe biden doesn't do that joe biden has  found or joe biden's plan does not  include higher tax brackets with higher  interest rates so right now if you you  would cap out around six hundred  thousand dollars with a thirty with  Biden's tax funded be thirty nine point  six percent you pay beyond six hundred  thousand dollars in earnings we probably  should have a tax bracket at forty  percent between six hundred thousand and  a million in earnings we should have a  forty five percent tax bracket between  one and three million  etc Joe Biden doesn't do that I think  that we need that and then number three  to the dismay of some people Joe Biden's  plan doesn't include  a wealth tax now I actually don't think  a wealth tax would ever pass so so while  it's an interesting idea I'm not going  crazy because Joe Biden doesn't include  it but if we look at the reality if we  look at the analysis that the Tax Policy  Center has done of this plan three  quarters of the increases fall on the  richest 1% and a whole bunch of that  other twenty five percent is on the top  five percent this is a very progressive  tax plan compared to what we've seen  certainly for the last 50 even a hundred  years in the United States I'm not  saying Joe Biden is the greatest period  but let's be honest and look at the tax  plan for what it is
 

